Single source of truth for the TODO vocabulary: the Cyberdeck TODO
aiu-context. Every `** STATE :AIU_CONTEXT_BODY:' heading there is a todo
state. Add, remove or reorder one there and `org-todo-keywords'
follows on the next reload — nothing else needs editing.

** AIU Context path
#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(defconst my/subnet-todo-aiu-context
  (expand-file-name
   "emacs-cyberdeck/files/aiu-registration/cyberdeck/aiu-context/todo"
   cyberdeck-emacs-loader-dir)
  "Canonical TODO vocabulary: the AIU Cyberdeck TODO AIU context.
The one place TODO states are defined. Adding, removing or
reordering a `** STATE :AIU_CONTEXT_BODY:' heading there changes
`org-todo-keywords' and `consult-todo-narrow' on the next reload.")
#+end_src

** Read the vocabulary
#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(defun my/subnet-todo-states ()
  "TODO states read from `my/subnet-todo-aiu-context', in file order.
Returns nil rather than signalling when the aiu-context is missing or
holds no body headings, so a renamed tree cannot break boot."
  (when (file-exists-p my/subnet-todo-aiu-context)
    (with-temp-buffer
      (insert-file-contents my/subnet-todo-aiu-context)
      (goto-char (point-min))
      (let (states)
        (while (re-search-forward
                "^\\*\\*+[ \t]+\\([A-Z][A-Z0-9-]*\\)[ \t]+:AIU_CONTEXT_BODY:"
                nil t)
          (let ((state (match-string 1)))
            (unless (member state states)
              (push state states))))
        (nreverse states)))))

(defun my/subnet-todo-keywords ()
  "Build the `org-todo-keywords' value from the aiu-context.
States before DONE are open; DONE and everything after it are
completed. With no DONE in the aiu-context every state is open.

Deliberately built from `member' and `length' alone. The obvious
`(position \"DONE\" states)' is `cl-position' or `seq-position'
depending on which library happens to be loaded, and is a void
function when neither is — which turns this into a boot-time error
that surfaces much later, in `org-set-regexps-and-options', a long way
from its cause."
  (let* ((states (or (my/subnet-todo-states) '("TODO" "DONE")))
         (done (member "DONE" states))
         ;; `member' returns the sublist starting at the match, DONE
         ;; included, so the length of that sublist is exactly DONE's
         ;; index. Subtracting (cdr done) instead would put DONE itself
         ;; on the open side of the separator.
         (split (if done
                    (- (length states) (length done))
                  (length states))))
    ;; `cons', not `list'. The entry must be (sequence . STATES) — a
    ;; dotted pair. `(list 'sequence STATES)' nests the states one level
    ;; too deep, and org's parser then hands that nested list straight to
    ;; `string-match', which is the "Wrong type argument: stringp" that
    ;; surfaced in 175 units at once.
    (list (cons 'sequence
                (append (cl-subseq states 0 split)
                        (list "|")
                        (cl-subseq states split))))))

(defun my/subnet-consult-todo-narrow ()
  "Build `consult-todo-narrow' from the aiu-context, one key per state.
The key is the state's first letter, then its two-letter prefix, then
its first letter plus a counter when both are taken — so every state
in the 24-word vocabulary gets a distinct, typeable selector. Returns
nil when the aiu-context yields no states."
  (let ((states (my/subnet-todo-states))
        (taken nil))
    (and states
         (mapcar
          (lambda (state)
            (let* ((lower (downcase state))
                   (key (or (seq-find (lambda (c) (not (member c taken)))
                                      (list (substring lower 0 1)
                                            (substring lower 0
                                                       (min 2 (length lower)))))
                            (format "%s%d" (substring lower 0 1)
                                    (length taken)))))
              (push key taken)
              (list (intern (concat "?" key)) state)))
          states))))
#+end_src

** Apply
`org-set-regexps-and-options' re-reads the vocabulary in every Org
buffer, so the sequence applies on load order and on file open
alike. This hook is the reason nothing downstream has to care
which unit happened to load first.
#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(with-eval-after-load 'org
  (setq org-todo-keywords (my/subnet-todo-keywords)))
(add-hook 'org-mode-hook #'org-set-regexps-and-options)
#+end_src
